From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomtomnet <tompere8000@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:15:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ui/sdl2: add an in-window control menu (Dear ImGui)

A menu bar at the top of the SDL window, like xemu's:

- Machine: pause/resume, send Ctrl+Alt+Del/Backspace/F1-F12, information
  about the VM, shut down (ACPI power button), reset and force off (both
  ask first).
- Snapshots: take, revert to and delete internal snapshots of the qcow2
  disks.  When the VM cannot be migrated (virtio-gpu with virgl, a mounted
  9p share, ...) its RAM cannot be saved: snapshots then hold the disks
  only, and reverting to one restarts the VM from them.  Writable disks
  that cannot be snapshotted (e.g. a raw OVMF_VARS) are left out instead
  of making every snapshot fail; the menu says which.
- USB: pass host USB devices through to the guest (usb-host) and back.
  Without access to a device node, the menu offers to get it: polkit
  (pkexec) asks for authentication and an ACL gives the user access to
  that node until the device is unplugged.  QEMU never runs as root.
- View: fullscreen, the menu bar, statistics and the menu size
  (automatic by default: the scale of the desktop).

On its right, the menu bar shows what the VM uses, once a second: vCPU
time, host GPU (from the DRM fdinfo of QEMU's own DRM clients: its
virglrenderer and window rendering), disk and network throughput (the
latter counted in the net layer for the guest's NICs), the guest frame
rate and the run state.  Details are in tooltips.

The information window lists the machine, accelerator, CPUs, memory,
firmware (UEFI or BIOS), QEMU version, graphics devices and their
acceleration (virgl, Venus, DRM native context), the virglrenderer in
use, the host GPU drivers, the window and guest display, disks with their
format and sizes, NICs and their backends, USB and audio devices, and it
can copy all of it to the clipboard.

Ctrl-Alt-M (the grab modifier plus M) shows and hides the menu bar.  Shown
that way it is docked: the guest display is drawn below it and the guest
is asked for a display of that size, like the GTK display does with its
menu bar.  Whether it is shown is remembered per VM (by -name, else
-uuid, else its first writable disk), and a VM that never ran starts with
the last choice.  Settings are kept in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qemu/sdl-gui.ini.

Letter hotkeys now follow the keyboard layout, as with the GTK display:
Ctrl-Alt-M is the key that types M, on AZERTY too, where the QWERTY
position of M types a comma.  Keys that type no ASCII character (non-Latin
layouts) keep their QWERTY position, and so do the digits.

In absolute pointer mode the grab now also follows the menu bar: it is
released over the bar as at the window border, so that the pointer can
leave the window through the top edge while the bar is shown.

While hidden the menu costs nothing: no frame is built, nothing is drawn.
Shown, it is drawn on top of the guest frames right before they are
presented; the window is only redrawn for the menu itself when it changed
and no guest frame came along to carry it.  Menu actions run from the
main loop, never while a frame is built or drawn.

Dear ImGui 1.92.9 is fetched as a meson subproject at configure time; its
OpenGL renderer uses libepoxy like the rest of QEMU.  The feature is
enabled automatically on Linux hosts when SDL and a C++ compiler are
available; --enable-sdl-gui / --disable-sdl-gui force it on or off.

Local patch, not for upstream submission: QEMU does not accept
AI-generated code (docs/devel/code-provenance.rst).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
